# ADR-0012: Parallel data rendering, MapReduce

## Context and Problem Statement

Current rendering implementation struggles to load large datasets efficiently. 
CSVs with 500k-1M data points take X amount of time to load, which is above
our maximum load time goal of 1 second.

## Decision Drivers

* Reduce rendering time for large datasets, with a target of  completing a draw within one second.
* Keep the main thread responsive while large datasets are rendered.
* Preserve existing chart features and deterministic series draw order.

## Considered Options

* Switching to WebGL
* MapReduce on canvas rendering

## Decision Outcome

Chosen option: **MapReduce on canvas rendering**, to avoid rewriting entire rendering engine 

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Series point data] --> B[Partition by series or point chunks]
  B --> C1[Worker 1: transform and render]
  B --> C2[Worker 2: transform and render]
  B --> Cn[Worker N: transform and render]
  C1 --> D[Worker-owned canvas layers]
  C2 --> D
  Cn --> D
  E[Main thread: background and axes] --> F[Composite layers in draw order]
  D --> F
  F --> G[Main thread: legend and labels]
  G --> H[Displayed chart]
```

### Consequences

* Bad: performance gains are not guaranteed; worker scheduling, data transfer,
  and combining results can outweigh parallel work.
## Pros and Cons of the Options

### Switching to WebGL

* Good: utilizes GPU speedup for graph rendering
* Good: supports fluid zooming and panning
* Bad: high learning curve, no one on the team has relavent experience
* Bad: requires rewrite of large potion of existing codebase

### MapReduce on canvas rendering

* Good: independent series or point chunks can have their coordinates
  prepared in parallel while retaining the Canvas 2D rendering approach.
* Good: moving CPU-heavy preparation off the main thread can keep the page
  responsive while large datasets are processed.
* Bad: workers cannot draw into the chart's existing Canvas 2D context. Using
  worker-owned canvases or `OffscreenCanvas` requires a composition step and
  care to preserve clipping and draw order.
* Bad: sending point data to workers and coordinating their results adds
  memory and messaging overhead, which may outweigh the gains for smaller
  datasets.
